  "findings": [
    "cap 30 is 1.16x cap 16 on the ClickBench hot sum (22.9s vs 26.6s).",
    "32 of 43 queries improve by >5% from cap 16 -> 30; ZERO regress by >5%.",
    "The 11 flat queries are the sub-250ms ones (q36-q42) -- latency-bound, cannot use more workers.",
    "A 32-core box at DOP 30 (22.9s) BEATS the published 192-core c8g.metal-48xl at DOP 16 (27.9s). The cap, not the hardware, was the binding constraint there.",
    "Parallel efficiency falls 100% -> 65% -> 40% across DOP 8/16/30. Doubling 8->16 bought 1.31x; nearly doubling again bought only 1.16x."
  ],
  "not_established": [
    "Whether the curve is still rising past DOP 30. The AWS account vCPU quota is exactly 32, so this box could not answer it; oversubscribing past 30 would confound the measurement.",
    "What the second wall IS. Efficiency at 40% by DOP 30 says something below 30 is already binding. Unidentified -- candidates are single-consumer egress (open j1-j4 SINKSERIAL item), group-by row-routing, and memory bandwidth.",
    "Any new DEFAULT for the cap. 30 was the best of three points on a visibly bending curve, and this says nothing about the <=8 vCPU Cloud Run prod target.",
    "Comparability of ABSOLUTE times to published ClickBench entries -- different runner and a shared load. Only the three caps against each other are clean. (The cap-16 arm landing within 5% of the published metal result is a sanity check that the runner is sane, not a like-for-like.)"
  ],
  "cross_check": {
    "our_cap16_hot_sum": 26.604,
    "published_c8g_metal_48xl_hot_sum": 27.9,
    "published_c8g_4xlarge_hot_sum": 30.7,
    "delta_pct_vs_published_metal": -4.6,
    "annotation": "Within 5% -- the private runner is not producing numbers from somewhere odd."
  }
